contributions.text:
Asked: with Black Market Connections already in for Midnight Reaper, the ONE next upgrade to threat-v1 and the card it replaces. The answer is Clever Concealment for Akroma's Will. The log's dominant loss is the wipe (001, 002, 006 — three of five losses, cause `wipe`), and 002's structural read still holds: 'my mana was spent casting vampires, so there was never open mana to hold up'. I enumerated the branch's protection by shape, not count: of seven protection-role cards (Akroma's Will, Boros Charm, Erebos, Giver of Runes, Selfless Spirit, Teferi's Protection, Yahenni), six are indestructible- or protection-shaped and stop only a destroy-wipe; exactly one — Teferi's Protection — survives the bounce Tom showed in 001 (Cyclonic Rift), a -X/-X wipe or an exile wipe. The branch also runs zero convoke spells (enumerated: none of the 64 non-lands). Clever Concealment is the one candidate that is both: a phase-out of every nonland permanent (tokens, Blood Artist, Elspeth, Bastion, Black Market Connections, Skullclamp) at instant speed, paid by tapping the white-and-black eminence tokens, needing no Edgar and no open mana. Akroma's Will is the cut because it is the same slot with the worse shape: its protection mode fails to the three wipe shapes Concealment stops, and its finisher mode — the one that makes it a wincon — is gated on controlling the commander, the exact gating the pilot removed from the suite on 2026-11-13. Removal reconciliation: my 'interaction closed, 11' was a role count, not a card count — see axes_engaged. Not the goldfish: no goldfish figure appears here, by the pilot's ruling.

add_candidates: [{"card": "Clever Concealment", "closes": "protection — by shape, not count: the branch's second phase-out (after Teferi's Protection), the one shape that beats a bounce, a -X/-X and an exile wipe, and the first protection the fuel pays for (card_roles.json files it protection:self, so the audit count moves 7 -> 7 with the Will cut)", "source": "prompt", "why": "Rank 1. {2}{W}{W} instant, convoke: any number of target nonland permanents you control phase out until your next turn. Edgar's eminence tokens are white AND black, so four untapped tokens cast it for nothing, at instant speed, on an opponent's turn, with every land tapped on Vampires — which is log 002's structural problem answered by the fuel itself. It protects what the thesis has been moving onto noncreatures and the creature wipes cannot reach but Rift, Farewell and Austere Command can: Bastion, Black Market Connections, Sanguine Bond, Anointed Procession, Caretaker's Talent, Skullclamp, plus Elspeth (log 005: 'protect the engine before pushing tempo') and Blood Artist (log 008: the table will 'remove Blood Artist early'). Against a single targeted removal spell it is a free fizzle on any engine piece. It needs no Edgar. The costs, said plainly: a phased-out board cannot block and its drainers miss opponents' deaths until it phases in; convoke taps the tokens Ashnod's Altar would otherwise sacrifice; tokens that attacked are tapped and cannot convoke, which the non-combat thesis mostly avoids. Not a Game Changer;

cut_candidates: [{"card": "Akroma's Will", "why": "Same slot as the add — a four-mana instant protection spell — with the worse shape. Its protection mode gives indestructible, lifelink and protection from each colour: that stops a destroy-wipe and targeted coloured removal, and does nothing against Cyclonic Rift (Tom, log 001), Toxic Deluge or Meathook-style -X/-X, or Farewell-style exile — the three shapes the branch covers only with Teferi's Protection. Its second mode, the flying double-strike alpha, is the reason it carries wincon:combat, and it is available only 'if you control a commander as you cast this spell' — the gating the pilot removed from the suite on 2026-11-13 (Deflecting Swat, Flawless Maneuver out). It costs four real mana at instant speed in a deck whose log says the mana is spent on Vampires. It is a combat finisher in a non-combat thesis (log 006: 'I want it to play more like life gain / life drain').", "cost_of_cutting": "A finisher, not just a protection card: with Edgar on the field both modes make the token board a flying, double-striking, lifelinking alpha, and every point of that lifelink is a drain through Vito, Sanguine Bond, Enduring Tenacity and Marauding Blight-Priest. The branch's wincon:combat count falls from 3 (Akroma's Will, Boros Charm, Edgar Ancient Bloodlord) to 2. It is also the branch's only protection that lets the creatures still BLOCK during the turn it protects them — a phased-out board cannot block, so Concealment is not an answer to an alpha
